  "question_origin": "researcher_constructed",
  "question_text": "Firm A sells its output in a perfectly competitive market for $4 per unit and hires workers in a perfectly competitive labor market for $12 per worker per day. Labor is its only variable input, and fixed cost is $20 per day. Workers must be hired in whole numbers. The production schedule is:\nWorkers L:         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nOutput Q per day:  0  10  18  24  28  30  31\n(a) Calculate the marginal revenue product of the second worker. Show your work.\n(b) Determine the profit-maximizing number of workers and explain using marginal analysis.\n(c) Calculate daily economic profit at that employment level. Show your work.\n(d) The wage rises to $20 per worker, with the output price unchanged. Determine the new profit-maximizing number of workers and explain using marginal analysis.\n(e) Return to the original $12 wage. Instead, the output price rises to $5. Draw a correctly labeled labor-market diagram for this firm's labor demand and its perfectly elastic labor supply, showing the effect of the output-price increase. Explain whether the firm's optimal whole-number employment changes.",
