      "answer": {
        "answer_markdown": "### (a) Marginal revenue product of the second worker\nThe second worker increases output from 10 to 18 units, so their marginal product is \\(18-10=8\\) units. In perfect competition, \\(MR=P=\\$4\\), hence:\n\\[\nMRP_2=MP_2\\times MR=8\\times\\$4=\\boxed{\\$32\\text{ per day}}.\n\\]\n\n### (b) Employment at the $12 wage\nHire workers while their MRP is at least the wage. The successive MRP values are \\(\\$40,\\$32,\\$24,\\$16,\\$8,\\$4\\). The fourth worker adds \\$16, exceeding the \\$12 wage; the fifth adds only \\$8, below the wage. Thus the firm hires **4 workers**.\n\n### (c) Daily economic profit\nAt 4 workers, output is 28 units and revenue is \\(28\\times\\$4=\\$112\\). Total cost is fixed cost plus labor cost: \\(\\$20+4(\\$12)=\\$68\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\pi=\\$112-\\$68=\\boxed{\\$44\\text{ per day}}.\n\\]\n\n### (d) Wage rises to $20\nAt the unchanged output price, the third worker's MRP is \\$24, while the fourth worker's is \\$16. Hire the third worker because \\(\\$24>\\$20\\), but not the fourth because \\(\\$16<\\$20\\). The new profit-maximizing employment is **3 workers**.\n\n### (e) Output price rises to $5\nThe higher output price raises each worker's MRP, shifting the firm's labor-demand curve (MRP) rightward. The perfectly elastic labor supply remains at the original wage of \\$12. At \\$5, the fourth worker's MRP is \\(4\\times\\$5=\\$20\\), above the wage, while the fifth worker's is \\(2\\times\\$5=\\$10\\), below it. The optimal whole-number employment therefore **increases from 4 to 4 workers?**\n\nCorrection: At the original price, the fourth worker's MRP is \\(4\\times\\$4=\\$16\\), so the firm hires four. At the higher price, the fifth worker's MRP is \\(2\\times\\$5=\\$10\\), still below \\$12; thus it continues to hire **4 workers**. The labor-demand curve shifts right, but the discrete employment choice does not change.",
