      "answer": {
        "answer_markdown": "**(a)** The second worker increases output from 10 to 18 units, so their marginal product is \\(18-10=8\\). Thus, \\(MRP_L=MP_L\\times P=8\\times\\$4=\\boxed{\\$32\\text{ per day}}\\).\n\n**(b)** At the original wage, hire workers while their marginal revenue product is at least the wage. The successive workers’ MRP values are \\$40, \\$32, \\$24, \\$16, \\$8, and \\$4. The fourth worker adds \\$16 in revenue against a \\$12 wage; the fifth adds only \\$8. Therefore, the profit-maximizing employment is **4 workers**.\n\n**(c)** At 4 workers, revenue is \\(28\\times\\$4=\\$112\\). Total cost is wages plus fixed cost: \\(4\\times\\$12+\\$20=\\$68\\). Economic profit is \\(\\$112-\\$68=\\boxed{\\$44\\text{ per day}}\\).\n\n**(d)** With a \\$20 wage, the first three workers have MRP values of \\$40, \\$32, and \\$24, each at least the wage; the fourth worker’s MRP is only \\$16. The firm therefore hires **3 workers**.\n\n**(e)** Labor demand is the firm’s MRP curve. Raising output price from \\$4 to \\$5 raises each worker’s MRP, shifting labor demand right/up. Labor supply to the firm remains perfectly elastic at the \\$12 wage. At \\$5, the successive workers’ MRP values are \\$50, \\$40, \\$30, \\$20, \\$10, and \\$5. The firm hires **4 workers**: the fourth worker is worthwhile, but the fifth is not. This is unchanged from the original optimal whole-number employment, even though labor demand shifts right.",
